I2C WORKFLOW: TRANSACTIONS AND SIDE EFFECTS

I2C1: /dev/i2c/1 (GP26 SDA, GP27 SCL).
Current i2cctl.c accepted command forms:
  i2cctl scan
  i2cctl read ADDR COUNT
  i2cctl write ADDR BYTE...
  i2cctl xfer ADDR READ_COUNT [BYTE...]
ADDR is 7-bit 0x08..0x77. COUNT is 1..32. Transfer size <=32.
Numerical arguments may use decimal or 0x-prefixed hex.

CAUTION: scan performs a REAL one-byte read at each address.
Some devices may respond with a side effect. Obtain approval before
scanning an unknown bus; do not repeat scans as polling.

EXAMPLE FOR A KNOWN, COMPATIBLE DEVICE ONLY
  i2cctl read 0x23 2
  i2cctl xfer 0x23 2 0x10
Those are syntax examples, NOT a universal sensor procedure.
Check the specific device's datasheet for address, register layout,
write/read ordering and conversion delay. Don't invent registers.

OUTPUT
  On success with a read length, i2cctl prints hexadecimal bytes.
  The caller must know sensor encoding and endianness before
  computing temperature, light level, etc.
  'transfer failed (N)' indicates ioctl failure; inspect wiring,
  power, device address and expected protocol, not just the code.

SAFER AGENT SEQUENCE
  pins -> identify sensor -> verify wiring/datasheet -> authorize
  -> one bounded read/transaction -> check output -> stop.
